<Rive version="1" kind="fragment">
    <!--
      The streak celebration's flame: a book lying flat, opening, with a flame rising out of
      the gutter.

      ORIENTATION, and this is the second attempt at it. The book lies on the floor and the
      spine runs away from the camera along Z, so the camera sits **at the book's own level**
      looking along the spine. What that shows is the *tail edge and nothing else*: the stacked
      edges of the pages, the edge of the cover boards under them, and a dark notch at the
      centre where the two halves part. The first version drew the page surfaces splaying open
      in a V, which is the view from above -- a camera looking down into the book. Wrong: from
      here the interior is edge-on and invisible. The drawing is a low horizontal slab.

      WHAT OPENING IS. A shut book seen down its spine is one board wide and its full
      thickness. Opened flat it is nearly two boards wide and about half as thick. So the whole
      transformation is two properties on one node -- `scaleX` 0.52 -> 1 and `scaleY` 1.9 -> 1
      -- plus the notch fading in, because a shut book has no visible gutter. That is also why
      there is no page-surface geometry left to fade or unfold: earlier drafts rotated the
      halves (which crossed them into a bow tie) and then cross-faded them (which spent the
      first fifth of the sequence as a grey slab). Neither problem exists in a drawing that
      never shows the inside.

      WHO OWNS THE MOTION, which changed too. **The easing lives in these timelines, and
      Flutter drives time linearly.** Previously the poses were six single-frame animations in
      a 1D blend state scrubbed by a bound number, and the consequence was that the file had no
      timeline at all: nothing to play in the editor, nothing to play in the previewer, nothing
      to iterate against. Every review had to go through a contact sheet, and a timing defect
      hid there for days -- the driving curve crossed the whole sequence in three frames and
      then held for 470ms, and no still image can show that.

      Now `Ignite` is an ordinary 46-frame timeline with cubic keyframes. It plays in the
      editor, it plays in `rive .`, and `--advance=250ms` samples it. Flutter seeks it by
      setting `Animation.time` (see `lib/ui/widgets/streak/streak_flame.dart`), so the host
      still decides *how far through* the sequence is -- which keeps reduced motion working
      with no special case and keeps the beat greppable in Dart -- while the *shape* of the
      motion is authored here, where it can be watched.

      There is deliberately **no state machine, no blend state and no view model**. None of
      them was earning anything once the timelines existed, and dropping them is what let the
      file shrink.

      `Idle` is the second timeline and it **loops**: the flame breathes and throws embers so
      it reads as alive rather than as a decal. Flutter advances it and mixes it in as the
      ignition lands. That reverses an earlier rule in this file which said nothing may move
      after the last beat; it was overruled deliberately, and the cost is real and worth
      stating -- an idle loop repaints for as long as the screen is up. It is a transient
      celebration with one way out rather than a screen left open, `Idle` is mixed to zero
      under reduced motion, and the host stops advancing it when it is not wanted.

      COLOURS are shipped tokens, not new ones. `library_card/card_lighting.dart` and
      `app_theme.dart` own them; this file has picked none of its own, because inventing brand
      colours is a mistake this repo has already made once.

      **The covers are near-black, and a teal version was built and then rejected.** Worth
      recording in full, because the argument for changing them was strong and still lost.

      `kCandleWellTop` / `kCandleWellBottom` were authored for the shareable library card — a
      dark candlelit scene where a near-black well is correct — and this artboard sits on cream.
      That makes the book the darkest thing in the frame by a wide margin, 14.4:1 against the
      ground and 9.3:1 against the flame body, where the Duolingo reference being chased
      contains no dark at all. So `AppColors.light.brandFill` #067657 was tried: a shipped
      token, 2.8:1 against the flame, close to orange's complement so the fire reads hotter,
      and it broke the drawing out of being monochrome amber.

      **A reader looked at it and preferred the dark covers.** Not a measurement anyone can
      re-derive, and it settles it — the contrast figures above describe what teal fixed, not
      whether the result was better. Keep them here so nobody re-runs the analysis and
      concludes the change was never considered.

      Two other candidates, both still ruled out on their own terms: `kCandleCoverLight`
      #D9A96B is already the far stop of the page-edge gradient below, so the covers would
      merge into the pages; `brand` #09BC8A measures 1.2:1 against the flame, as bright as the
      fire, so the boards stop reading as the thing being burnt on.
        FFFFD479  flame core     - the record's lit-today highlight
        FFF2A93F  flame body     - kCandleFlame
        FFB54708  flame edge     - AppColors.light.flame
        FF26190A  the striations  - kCandleStockTop, at 18%
        FF35230E / FF1B1106  cover board edge - kCandleWellTop / kCandleWellBottom
        FF130D05  the gutter's floor - kCandleStockBottom
        FFFDFAF1  page edges, lit    - the record's paper
        FFFFE8C4  page edges, falling off - kCandleGlow
        FFD9A96B  page edges, far    - kCandleCoverLight
        FFFFF3DD  the flame's hot tip - kShareChromeLitInk

      The ground this sits on is kCandleGlow #FFE8C4, painted by `streak_celebration.dart`.
      A *lighter* halo is invisible on it, so the glow below is warmer rather than brighter.
    -->
    <Artboard styleId="0:5" defaultStateMachineId="0:1264" width="360" height="300" name="Flame" id="0:2">
        <LayoutComponentStyle name="Artboard Style" id="0:5"/>

        <!-- No background Fill: the celebration paints its own cream ground, and a filled
             artboard would sit on it as a visible panel. `sheet.py` patches a copy of this
             project to composite against that cream rather than the previewer's #1D1D1D,
             which is why no preview-only shape ships here any more. -->

        <!-- Draw order is declaration order, FRONT FIRST. -->

        <!-- ============================ THE FIRE ============================ -->

        <!-- The living embers, which belong to `Idle` and not to the ignition. Separate shapes
             from `burst` on purpose: the burst is a one-off that must be *gone* at rest, and
             reusing it would mean the resting frame could never be still.

             **Declared in front of the fire, which is not a detail -- it is why the loop was
             invisible.** They were behind it, spawning around the tip and rising through the
             body, which is exactly the region the flame occupies; every one of them was
             occluded for its whole flight, and the first render of `Idle` showed a flame
             breathing on its own with no spray at all. Nothing else about the timeline was
             wrong. Drawn in front they read as sparks carried off the tip, which is what they
             are.

             **All five are `kCandleFlame` #F2A93F, and that reverses an earlier rule here.**
             When the embers were *behind* the flame the note said never to use #B54708 because
             an ember the colour of the body it just left is invisible -- and the same argument
             now rules out the two pale tokens, because in front of the flame an ember spends
             almost its whole life over the #FFE8C4 cream ground, where #FFD479 is a four-point
             difference and simply is not there. #F2A93F is the one token in the set that reads
             against both the ground and the core. The first tuned version used the pale pair at
             three to five pixels and rendered as dust on the lens.

             **Each is invisible at both ends of its flight, and that is what makes the loop
             seamless.** Rive holds a keyed property at its *first* keyframe's value for every
             frame before it, so an ember whose window is 14..40 simply sits at its birthplace
             from 0 to 14 and at its death place from 40 to 72 -- and jumps back at the seam
             while its opacity is zero. No snap-back keyframes are needed, and the earlier ones
             (`frame 45 value -34` and friends) were noise. -->
        <Node x="180" y="270" name="fire" id="0:20">

            <!-- The shimmer sweep, clipped to the flame's own silhouette: the point is that
                 the flame gleams, not that a white stripe crosses the screen. Authored
                 invisible for the same reason as the burst.

                 **26.5 and not 20, because the band's width is absolute and the body's is
                 not.** `smooth.py`'s `SCALE_X` went 1.8 -> 2.3869 to reach Phosphor's aspect,
                 which widened the body by a third and left this at 40 units -- so the sweep
                 fell from 29% of the silhouette to 22% and stopped reading as a gleam. On a
                 narrow flame a thin band is a highlight; on a broad one it is a crease, and
                 the render of the settle at 1100ms showed exactly that: a hard diagonal line
                 down the left shoulder that looks like a fold in the drawing. Scaled by the
                 same 1.326 it holds its old share, which is the number this was chosen at.

                 Note `fire.scaleX` does *not* need accounting for: this shape is a child of
                 `fire`, so the burst scales the band and the body together and the ratio is
                 constant through the animation. Only a change to the *path* moves it. -->
            <Shape y="-120" rotation="-0.45" opacity="0" name="gleam" id="0:23">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:260">
                    <StraightVertex x="-26.5" y="-198" id="0:261"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="26.5" y="-198" id="0:262"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="26.5" y="198" id="0:263"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-26.5" y="198" id="0:264"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:265">
                    <LinearGradient startX="-26.5" startY="0" endX="26.5" endY="0" name="Gradient" id="0:266">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="00FDFAF1" position="0" id="0:267"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="6BFDFAF1" position="0.5" id="0:268"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="00FDFAF1" position="1" id="0:269"/>
                    </LinearGradient>
                </Fill>
                <ClippingShape sourceId="0:25" name="Clip" id="0:270"/>
            </Shape>

            <!-- The bright core. Its own shape rather than a lighter fill, so it can lick
                 independently of the body: `rotation` on this and not on the body is the flame
                 moving rather than the whole flame tilting. -->
            <Shape y="3" name="flame_inner" id="0:24">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:271">
                    <StraightVertex x="0" y="-110.75" radius="9" id="0:272"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="24.8238" y="-75.65" inRotation="4.2677" inDistance="13.82" outRotation="1.1261" outDistance="13.82" id="0:273"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="35.6842" y="-35.87" inRotation="4.7352" inDistance="11.31" outRotation="1.5937" outDistance="11.31" id="0:274"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="23.2723" y="-7.79" inRotation="5.4896" inDistance="8.48" outRotation="2.3481" outDistance="8.48" id="0:275"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="0" y="0.4" inRotation="0.0000" inDistance="7.76" outRotation="3.1416" outDistance="7.76" id="0:276"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-23.2723" y="-7.79" inRotation="0.7935" inDistance="8.48" outRotation="3.9351" outDistance="8.48" id="0:277"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-35.6842" y="-35.87" inRotation="1.5479" inDistance="11.31" outRotation="4.6895" outDistance="11.31" id="0:278"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-24.8238" y="-75.65" inRotation="2.0155" inDistance="13.82" outRotation="5.1571" outDistance="13.82" id="0:279"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:280">
                    <!-- **Flat, and it was a gradient for every revision before this one.** The
                         core ramped #FFF3DD to #FFD479 down its own height, which is what you
                         draw when you are rendering fire. A reader's verdict on the rendered
                         version was that it looked unrefined next to Duolingo's, and the single
                         largest difference between the two is not shape -- it is that theirs is
                         two flat colours and ours was four gradient stops across two shapes.
                         Flat fills read as illustration; ramps read as an attempt at realism
                         that did not get there. -->
                    <SolidColor colorValue="FFFFD479" name="C" id="0:281"/>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>

            <!-- The body. **A sharp apex, a base wide enough to sit in the gutter, and the
                 widest point low.**
                 A flame is widest about a quarter of the way up and tapers over the whole
                 upper two thirds to a leaning tip. Four mirrored vertices gave an egg; putting
                 the widest point too low and the base as wide as the shoulders gave a tulip,
                 flaring and pinching and flaring again; six evenly spaced ones with the widest
                 at mid-height gave a gothic leaf with near-parallel sides and a maroon pinch
                 where it met the book. Eight, with a *shoulder* between the apex and the belly
                 and a base wide enough to sit down in the gutter rather than balance on it, is
                 what finally read as fire. The apex is a `StraightVertex` so the curves meet in
                 a corner rather than rounding over, and it sits right of centre so the whole
                 silhouette leans. Gradient-filled, because a flat fill is what made the
                 hand-built version read as a sticker.

                 **The handles are `rive/streak_flame/smooth.py`'s output, not hand-tuned, and
                 that is what fixed the faceting.** A mirrored vertex spends one angle and one
                 length on both of its segments, so a tangent chosen to suit one neighbour kinks
                 the other; detached vertices let the script fit a Catmull-Rom tangent through
                 each point instead. Move a point there and paste, rather than nudging numbers
                 here.

                 **The ramp is held flat for the top half and only falls to `flame` at the
                 base.** Run linearly over the whole height -- which is how this started -- the
                 midtone is a brown-orange nothing, the silhouette has no value contrast
                 against the core, and the result is the soft airbrushed blob the reader
                 called plain. A flat saturated body with a burnt foot is both bolder and more
                 like what fire does. -->
            <Shape name="flame_outer" id="0:25">
                <PointsPath isClosed="true" isClockwise="true" name="Path" id="0:285">
                    <StraightVertex x="19.0952" y="-237.2" radius="18" id="0:286"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="78.7677" y="-140" inRotation="4.2962" inDistance="31.49" outRotation="1.1546" outDistance="31.49" id="0:287"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="95.476" y="-64.4" inRotation="4.9582" inDistance="22.89" outRotation="1.8166" outDistance="22.89" id="0:288"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="45.3511" y="-6.8" inRotation="5.6615" inDistance="19.57" outRotation="2.5199" outDistance="19.57" id="0:289"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="0" y="4" inRotation="0.0397" inDistance="15.13" outRotation="3.1813" outDistance="15.13" id="0:290"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-45.3511" y="-10.4" inRotation="0.7080" inDistance="19.38" outRotation="3.8496" outDistance="19.38" id="0:291"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-88.3153" y="-71.6" inRotation="1.4150" inDistance="23.08" outRotation="4.5566" outDistance="23.08" id="0:292"/>
                    <CubicDetachedVertex x="-66.8332" y="-147.2" inRotation="1.8334" inDistance="22.99" outRotation="4.9750" outDistance="22.99" id="0:293"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-52.5118" y="-204.8" radius="14" id="0:299"/>
                    <StraightVertex x="-23.869" y="-183.2" radius="9" id="0:300"/>
                </PointsPath>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:294">
                    <!-- **One flat colour, and the burnt foot is gone entirely.** It ramped to
                         `flame` #B54708 over the last 12% -- itself a correction from the last
                         48%, which had put the drawing's darkest value exactly where the flame
                         meets a near-black cover. The remaining sliver went the same way for
                         the same reason a reader gave about the whole thing: **there is no dark
                         anywhere in Duolingo's flame.** A burnt base is an observation about
                         real fire, and this is not a picture of real fire.

                         This reverses the oldest note in this file, which said a flat saturated
                         body with a burnt foot is bolder than a linear ramp and more like what
                         fire does. Both halves of that were true and the second one stopped
                         being the goal. -->
                    <SolidColor colorValue="FFF2A93F" name="C" id="0:295"/>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>

            <!-- The ignition burst. **Generated, not authored: see `burst.py`.** Twelve bits,
                 each with its own flight, its own clock and its own morph, drawn from one seeded
                 RNG. Everything below this comment down to the closing `</Node>`, plus the
                 `KeyedObject`s for it in `Ignite`, is that script's output; edit the recipe there
                 and re-run, do not nudge numbers here.

                 **It is declared after `flame_outer`, which is deliberate.** Draw order is
                 declaration order, front first, so this sits *behind* both flame shapes and a bit
                 is visible only where it has cleared the silhouette. It used to be the first
                 child of `fire`, i.e. frontmost, and a reader called the result measles. The
                 count was part of it -- ten dots -- but the real defect was the timing, and it
                 was invisible until the two numbers were put side by side: opacity peaked at
                 **0.95 on frame 61** while the node's scale was still only **0.41**, so ten pale
                 discs lit up at their brightest while they were still bunched on top of the body.
                 Behind the flame, overlap is self-solving rather than something to tune around.

                 **What replaced the six dots, and why.** A reader compared the spray to a
                 Duolingo still: "the shapes morph, and initial position and travel paths of each
                 are more random". The old six were children of this node while it carried
                 `scaleX`/`scaleY` 0.85 -> 1.35 and one shared opacity envelope -- so they left on
                 the same radial line at the same speed, brightened and died on the same frame,
                 were circles within 2 units of one size, and sat in near-mirrored pairs (+-76,
                 +-84, +-58). The `embers` group below already did the opposite, per-particle, so
                 this was the odd one out rather than the file lacking a pattern.

                 **Morph is `width`/`height`/`cornerRadiusTL` on each `Rectangle`, never
                 `scaleX`/`scaleY` on its `Shape`.** Scaling the node is a zoom: the corner radius
                 scales with everything else, so a rounded square stays the same rounded square
                 and only gets bigger. Keying the parametric path turns a bar into a stub and a
                 pill into a rounded square, which is the thing the reference is doing.

                 **This node no longer carries `opacity="0"`, and that is load-bearing.** Rive
                 multiplies a parent's opacity into its children, so with the reveal moved onto
                 the individual bits an authored-invisible group renders an empty frame -- which
                 looks exactly like a broken keyframe table. Each `Shape` is authored at 0
                 instead, the way `embers` does it, so the resting artboard still wears no spray.

                 **The artboard is 360 wide because of this spray.** At 300 square the field
                 between the settled silhouette (half-width 93) and the frame's edge (150) was 57
                 units, and 10 at the burst peak, so bits placed for the old narrower flame spent
                 their whole flight occluded. Widening to 360 puts the reach at 1.94 flame
                 half-widths against the reference's estimated 2.0-2.5, and it costs nothing on
                 screen because the box grew with it -- see `StreakFlame.artboardAspect`. Bits go
                 sideways and up; there is no room below (the book) and little above (the burst's
                 tip clears the top by 19).

                 **Only the two bright tokens and never `flame` #B54708**: some of these cross a
                 book cover, and an ember the colour of what it left is an ember nobody can see.
                 The mix is weighted toward `#F2A93F` because these now sit on white paper rather
                 than on the flame, and `#FFD479` nearly disappears there. -->
            <Node y="-48" name="burst" id="0:21">
