        <!--
          The light, behind the thing making it. **A flattened pool on the table now, not a
          halo behind the flame**, and the change was forced by the ground going white.

          It used to be a 520-unit circle of `kCandleFlame` centred behind the flame's middle.
          On the cream `#FFE8C4` ground that was nearly invisible, which is why the note here
          used to say the halo is warmer rather than brighter -- a *lighter* halo on cream is
          not there at all. On white the opposite problem appeared: the same shape reads as a
          large diffuse smudge behind the flame, and it was immediately the least convincing
          element on the screen. Duolingo's streak flame has no halo whatsoever; what it has is
          a pale pool directly beneath the flame, which grounds the object and reads as spill in
          a single shape.

          **It stays backmost, and that is what makes it safe.** The tempting move is to draw
          the pool *on* the paper, in front of the book -- light falls on paper, after all. But
          the book's boards are a near-black bar across y 454..469, exactly where a pool
          belongs, and a translucent warm shape fading in over them is the grey-mud failure this
          file has hit five times. Left behind the book, the book simply sits *in* the pool: the
          boards occlude its middle and what shows is the light spreading past them onto the
          table. Same read, no compositing risk, and no reordering.

          `kCandleFlame` #FFD479 rather than the ground token, because the pool has to be warmer
          than *both* things it lies on -- the white table and the book's near-white paper. A
          cream pool on cream paper is nothing, which is the same mistake as the halo on cream,
          one layer down.

          **The ellipse and its gradient are circular, and `pool` flattens them. Do not flatten
          the ellipse itself.** A `RadialGradient` has one radius vector, so on a 420x116 path
          the gradient cannot fade inside the short axis: alpha is still around 60% where the
          path ends, and the ellipse's own boundary shows up as a hard horizontal arc running
          the full width of the frame. It reads as a horizon, or a hill behind the book, and it
          was worse than the halo it replaced. Rendered and rejected once. Scaling a circle
          keeps the falloff isotropic in its own space, so the pool has no edge anywhere.
        -->
        <Node x="180" y="260" scaleY="0.18" name="pool" id="0:36">
            <Shape opacity="0.58" name="glow" id="0:50">
                <Ellipse width="340" height="340" name="Path" id="0:405"/>
                <Fill name="Fill" id="0:406">
                    <RadialGradient startX="0" startY="0" endX="0" endY="170" name="Gradient" id="0:407">
                        <GradientStop colorValue="B3FFD479" position="0" id="0:408"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="80FFD479" position="0.5" id="0:409"/>
                        <GradientStop colorValue="00FFD479" position="1" id="0:410"/>
                    </RadialGradient>
                </Fill>
            </Shape>
        </Node>


        <!-- The easing. Authored here rather than in Dart, so the timeline the editor plays is
             the motion that ships. -->
        <CubicEaseInterpolator x1="0.16" y1="1" x2="0.3" y2="1" id="0:200"/>
        <CubicEaseInterpolator x1="0.45" y1="0" x2="0.55" y2="1" id="0:201"/>
        <CubicEaseInterpolator x1="0.34" y1="1.56" x2="0.64" y2="1" id="0:202"/>
        <CubicEaseInterpolator x1="0.55" y1="0" x2="1" y2="0.45" id="0:203"/>
        <!-- A *mild* ease-out, for the embers. `0:200` is a (0.16,1,0.3,1) snap that is right
             for something arriving and wrong for something drifting: a quarter of the way
             through its flight an ember was already four fifths of the way up, so the spray
             appeared as specks pinned near the top of the frame with a gap between them and the
             flame they supposedly came off. A spark slows as it cools; it does not stop. -->
        <CubicEaseInterpolator x1="0.33" y1="0.5" x2="0.7" y2="1" id="0:204"/>

        <!--
          ============================ IGNITE ============================
          78 frames at 60fps = 1300ms, one shot. Flutter seeks this by time; the editor plays
          it. The beats:

            0 .. 6    the shut book, HELD. Without this hold the reader never saw a closed
                      book at all: `backOut` front-loads so hard that at frame 4 -- 66ms --
                      the covers were already 70% of the way open, and the whole point of the
                      sequence is that a book opens. A held pose is the cheapest possible fix
                      and the only one that works, because the curve is what gives the landing
                      its knock and softening it costs that.
            6 .. 30   the cover swings. `leaf` rotates -177 degrees about the spine, sweeping
                      up and over; `base` tilts the matching 3 degrees the other way so the
                      pair lands in a shallow V; the spine squashes to half height and becomes
                      the gutter; and the whole book slides right so the spine finishes centred
                      under the flame. Four properties, no opacity, nothing to composite.
           20 .. 34   the room lights, and only to 0.75. **Not from frame 0.** It used to,
                      which lit a warm halo behind a book that was still shut and nothing was
                      burning -- light before its source, visible in every contact sheet and
                      noticed in none of them for three passes.
           22 .. 30   a spark lifts out of the gutter and settles as a *small* flame, 0.44 of
                      the size it finishes at. That smallness is the whole setup.
           34 .. 54   **the strain.** Two attempts to grow, each one pushing up and narrowing
                      and then being dragged back down -- but dragged back to a little more
                      than it left, so the trend is upward and the failure is not a reset.
                      Underneath, `fire.x` judders +-1.4px on `linear` keys: a tremor, and the
                      thing a haptic pairs with.
           54 .. 60   **the burst.** 100ms, which is the second thing here that had to be
                      corrected: it was 67ms on `easeOut`, and `easeOut` is so front-loaded
                      that 85% of the growth landed in the *first frame of four*. Rendered
                      frame by frame that is one cell of small flame followed by one cell of
                      large flame -- a cut, not a burst, with no gesture in between. Six frames
                      on the milder `0:204` gives five distinct sizes on the way up, which is
                      what makes it read as erupting. Fast and instantaneous are not the same
                      thing, and at 60fps the difference is three frames.
           60 .. 78   it draws back down -- undershooting to 0.94 and easing back -- to a
                      lively flame about 2.2x the one it strained from.
           78         everything has settled, so `Idle` can take over with nothing to correct.

          **The strain and the burst replaced a single monotonic rise**, and not because the
          rise was broken. It was legible and it was inert: the flame simply got bigger over
          22 frames and arrived. The brief is the Duolingo feel -- *strains to grow, with
          haptics, then bursts with large fire, then grows down again but larger than before
          to a lively flame* -- and that shape cannot be expressed by a curve on one segment,
          because the whole point is that the first two attempts **fail**. It needs the
          failures keyed.

          **Haptics go at the two strain peaks and the burst**, i.e. 633ms, 767ms and 967ms,
          with the light rumble spanning 567..900ms alongside the tremor. Recorded here so the
          Dart side has somewhere to read it from; nothing is wired yet.

          **The body's squash is NOT counter-phased against the node's during the burst.** The
          obvious construction -- `fire` stretches tall, `flame_outer` also stretches tall for
          a secondary -- multiplies, because they are ancestor and descendant: 1.48 x 1.18 is
          1.75 and the fireball came out as a tall thin spike, the opposite of bursting. So
          `flame_outer` *balloons* at the peak (1.14 wide x 0.94 tall) to round the product off
          to roughly 1.41 x 1.39, then over-narrows on the rebound. Same principle for
          `flame_inner`: it peaks at frame 58, two frames *ahead* of the body, rather than
          bigger than it -- a core that leads in time reads as a core that leads, and a core
          scaled past its own body pokes out the top of it.

          The rotation is **negative**, and that is not arbitrary: at +90 degrees the leaf
          sweeps *down* through the table before coming up the other side. Sign it the other way
          and the book opens by passing its cover through the floor.
        -->
        <!-- ======================== THE STATE MACHINE ========================= -->
        <!--
          **This exists so a human can watch the whole thing once**, and for no other reason.

          The app never runs it. `_FlamePainter` is a `BasicArtboardPainter`, which stores the
          artboard and calls `artboard.advance` and nothing else — it instantiates no state
          machine — and the two timelines are driven by hand: `Ignite` is *seeked* by `progress`
          because the celebration's clock lives in Dart, and `Idle` is advanced and mixed at
          `liveness`. None of that changes with a machine present, which is what makes adding one
          safe rather than a second source of truth. (`StateMachinePainter`, in the same file,
          *would* have run it. Do not switch to it.)

          What it buys is the review loop. Without a `defaultStateMachineId` the editor and the
          CLI previewer fall back to the artboard's *first animation*, so the sequence could only
          ever be watched in halves — `Ignite`, then separately `Idle` — and a reader asked for
          the obvious thing, which is to press play once and see the flame catch and then settle.
          This file's own history is the argument: the timelines themselves exist because an
          earlier version had none, so nothing played it and every review went through a contact
          sheet.

          `exitTimeIsPercetange` **is misspelled in the format itself** and the correct spelling
          is rejected. Write the typo. With `exitTime="100"` it means "leave only once `Ignite`
          has played all the way through", which is how timelines chain into a sequence;
          `duration="0"` because the two poses already meet — `Ignite`'s last frame returns every
          transform to its authored value, which is the same rest pose `Idle` oscillates around,
          so there is nothing to blend.

          `AnyState` and `ExitState` are unused and **mandatory anyway**: a layer missing any of
          the three built-in states does not import. Every state carries an `x`/`y` for the same
          kind of reason — left unset they all default to (0, 0), stack on one point in the
          editor's graph, and `inspect` reports `states-overlap`.

          **`_preview.py` strips `defaultStateMachineId` from its copies.** The preview scripts
          depend on the first-animation fallback this removes: `motion.py`, `apex.py` and
          `flicker.py` all reach `Idle` by reordering the timelines so it comes first, which a
          running machine would simply ignore — it would play `Ignite` and then `Idle`, and a
          request for "Idle frame 20" would silently render something else. So the shipped file
          has a machine and the render copies do not.
        -->
        <StateMachine name="Play" id="0:1264">
            <StateMachineLayer name="Layer 1" id="0:1265">
                <AnyState x="520" y="-120" id="0:1266"/>
                <ExitState x="680" y="-120" id="0:1267"/>
                <EntryState x="0" y="0" id="0:1268">
                    <StateTransition stateToId="0:1270" id="0:1269"/>
                </EntryState>
                <AnimationState x="160" y="0" animationId="0:60" id="0:1270">
                    <StateTransition stateToId="0:1272" duration="0" enableExitTime="true"
                                     exitTimeIsPercetange="true" exitTime="100" id="0:1271"/>
                </AnimationState>
                <AnimationState x="340" y="0" animationId="0:61" id="0:1272"/>
            </StateMachineLayer>
        </StateMachine>

        <LinearAnimation fps="60" duration="78" loopValue="oneShot" name="Ignite" id="0:60">

            <!-- The cover, up and over. `backOut` so it lands with a knock rather than easing
                 to a halt, and a `hold` in front of it so there is a book to watch open.

                 The four keys after 30 are the **flex under the burst**: the pair flattens a
                 little just before the fireball arrives and deepens under it, then returns.
                 Both halves move the same way in the V even though their numbers move in
                 opposite directions, because the leaf is flipped -- -3.115 rad is 1.5 degrees
                 off flat and -3.075 is 3.8, so *more* negative is *shallower* on this one and
                 deeper on `base`. Get that backwards and the book scissors. -->
            <KeyedObject objectId="0:42" id="0:734">
                <KeyedProperty propertyKey="15" id="0:735">
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="0" value="0" interpolationType="hold" id="0:736"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="6" value="0" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:202" id="0:737"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="30" value="-3.0892" interpolationType="hold" id="0:738"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="48" value="-3.0892" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:900"/>
