            <!--
              The fire. Three phases on one node, and the numbers are the choreography:

                22 .. 30   a spark becomes a small flame          0.25x0.18 -> 0.44x0.46
                34 .. 54   strain, twice, gaining ground          -> 0.44x0.60, -> 0.45x0.74
                54 .. 60   burst                                  -> 1.24x1.48
                60 .. 78   settle                                 -> 1.00x1.00

              scaleX and scaleY are **counter-phased through the strains**: each push up
              narrows the flame and each collapse widens it, which is what makes the two
              attempts read as effort rather than as a stepped fade-in. They are *not*
              counter-phased on the burst -- both grow, because a burst is a burst.

              Each failure is dragged back to slightly more than it left (0.50 then 0.60, off
              a 0.46 start) so the trend under the sawtooth is upward. Pull those back level
              and the flame reads as stuck rather than as straining.

              `fire.x` is the tremor: +-1.4px on `linear` keys through the strain only, which
              is deliberately cheap -- a tremor is a vibration, and an eased vibration is a
              wobble. It is the visual half of the haptic; the keys are two frames apart, i.e.
              30Hz, fast enough to blur on a real screen.

              `fire.y` ends at 228, the authored value, and not at the 230 it strains from.
              **The artboard's authored pose is the resting pose** -- see the artboard's own
              note -- so every property this timeline touches has to land back on it or
              scrubbing the file in the editor shows a flame parked somewhere the app never
              puts it.
            -->
