            </KeyedObject>
        </LinearAnimation>

        <!--
          ============================= IDLE =============================
          72 frames at 60fps = 1.2s, looping.

          **Every property starts and ends on the same value, or is invisible at both ends.**
          Otherwise the loop has a visible seam once a second for as long as the screen is up.
          The embers take the second route: Rive holds a keyed property at its first
          keyframe's value for every frame before it, so an ember sits at its birthplace until
          its window opens and at its death place afterwards, and the jump back happens at zero
          opacity.

          **It may only key properties `Ignite` leaves at their authored values.** Flutter
          applies the ignition and then mixes this in on top, so anything keyed here *replaces*
          what the ignition left. The flame's and core's scales and the glow's scale all end the
          ignition at 1 and are safe. The glow's *opacity* is not -- `Ignite` settles it at 0.58
          -- so this timeline does not touch it, and must not start to.

          **Two breaths per loop, not one, and unequal.** A single 1.2s swell at three percent
          was below the threshold of being seen at all: the first render of this loop was
          indistinguishable from a still. Fire moves faster and less regularly than a
          breathing chest, so the amplitudes and the spacing are deliberately uneven -- an
          even two-cycle sine is a pulsing logo.
        -->
        <LinearAnimation fps="60" duration="72" loopValue="loop" name="Idle" id="0:61">

            <!-- **The body barely changes shape at all now, in either direction.** Two
                 corrections landed here, a round apart, and the second undid most of what the
                 first left standing.

                 It began as `scaleX` 0.955..1.07 against `scaleY` 0.945..1.06 in antiphase --
                 volume-preserving squash and stretch, which is how you animate a bouncing ball
                 and the wrong physics for fire. Measured, the apex rose and fell 25px while the
                 belly swelled 15px: 1.67 to 1, so the flame was breathing sideways almost as
                 much as it was rising. `scaleX` went nearly flat for that, and `scaleY` came
                 *down* rather than up -- the counter-intuitive half, since raising it is the
                 obvious way to buy vertical travel and it lifts the belly along with the tip.

                 Then a reader named the remaining problem exactly: Duolingo's flame flickers,
                 ours bounced. A whole silhouette swelling and shrinking on a slow even period
                 *is* a bounce, at any amplitude -- so both axes came down again, to about a
                 percent. What is left is not meant to be seen as motion; it is there because a
                 rigid body under a flickering tip reads as a mask with something moving behind
                 it. All the visible movement is now in the tip vertices at the bottom of this
                 timeline, where it can be irregular. -->
            <KeyedObject objectId="0:25" id="0:515">
                <KeyedProperty propertyKey="16" id="0:516">
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="0" value="1" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:517"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="10" value="1.008" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:518"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="22" value="0.994" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:519"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="38" value="1.006" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:520"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="54" value="0.996" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:521"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="64" value="1.003" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:522"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="72" value="1" id="0:523"/>
                </KeyedProperty>
                <KeyedProperty propertyKey="17" id="0:524">
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="0" value="1" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:525"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="10" value="0.988" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:526"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="22" value="1.014" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:527"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="38" value="0.991" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:528"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="54" value="1.011" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:529"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="64" value="0.995" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:530"/>
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="72" value="1" id="0:531"/>
                </KeyedProperty>
            </KeyedObject>

            <!-- The core, out of step with the body so the two do not pulse together.

                 **Its sideways motion is cut to a third of what it was.** Rotation here is
                 radians, so the old ±0.07 was ±4 degrees of lean on the brightest shape in the
                 drawing, and the old `x` slid it ±1.8 units on top of that. Between them the
                 core's tip was the most horizontally restless thing on screen -- and it sits
                 *inside* the silhouette, so all that motion read as the flame wobbling rather
                 than as anything licking. A trace is kept rather than none, because the
                 asymmetry is what stops the two shapes looking welded.

                 **`scaleY` came down too, and an earlier version of this comment was wrong to
                 say it was "already the right kind of motion".** It was the right *axis* and
                 the wrong rhythm: 0.93..1.08 on four even eased beats pumped the core like a
                 lung, and a reader reading the whole thing as bouncing rather than flickering
                 was reading this as much as the body. It is now ±4%, and the core's tip does
                 the moving -- see `CORE_LICK` at the bottom of this timeline.

                 The reader's note was explicitly about the inner flame as well as the outer
                 one, which is why the core got its own keyed tip rather than being left to
                 inherit the body's. -->
            <KeyedObject objectId="0:24" id="0:532">
                <KeyedProperty propertyKey="15" id="0:533">
                    <KeyFrameDouble frame="0" value="0" interpolationType="cubic" interpolatorId="0:201" id="0:534"/>
