            <!--
              The tips flicker. **These key path vertices directly, which nothing else here
              does**, and it is the only way to move the top of a flame without moving the rest
              of it: a transform on `flame_outer` scales the belly by the same factor as the
              apex, so every attempt at "more vertical motion" through `scaleY` also inflates
              the shape. `Vertex::y` is animatable (`rive schema StraightVertex`, key 25), so a
              tip can rise and fall against a body that holds still.

              **Both flames have a keyed tip, not just the outer one.** The reader's note was
              about the inner flame too, and a core that only scales is a shape breathing inside
              a shape that flickers.

              **The beats are irregular, fast and `linear`, and that is the whole difference
              between flickering and bouncing.** The first version of this gave each tip four
              evenly-spaced beats on the same eased curve as everything else, and the verdict
              was that Duolingo's flame flickers while ours bounced. Amplitude was never the
              problem: four smooth symmetric beats over 1.2s is a 1.7Hz sine, and a sine is a
              bounce however tall it is, because the eye can predict where the tip goes next.
              What changed is rhythm -- about three times as many beats, at uneven gaps, with
              amplitudes that vary beat to beat and straddle zero, and no easing, so every beat
              arrives with a corner in it. Same reasoning as the embers' opacity a few blocks
              up, which is linear because an eased fade on something four pixels across reads as
              a curve where a flicker is wanted.

              **The numbers come out of `smooth.py`, not out of here** --
              `../../.venv/bin/python rive/streak_flame/smooth.py`, under `Idle: the tips'
              lick`. They are the scaled form of small `(frame, dy)` tables in raw silhouette
              units, so a change to `SCALE_Y` -- or, for the core, to `CORE_SHRINK` -- moves the
              loop with the shape. Hand-written, -253.4 would be correct at 1.8x and a mystery
              at any other scale, which is the trap `IGNITE_FRAMES` and the 304x304 preview
              ground both fell into. Do not edit these by hand; edit the tables and re-run.

              On the outer flame, index 9 is deliberately *not* keyed: it is the bottom of the
              crease, and moving it with 8 would slide the notch bodily instead of letting the
              lick rise out of it.

              **The tangent blocks are not a separate idea, they are the other half of this
              one.** A Catmull-Rom tangent at vertex `i` is computed from `i-1` and `i+1`, so
              moving a tip invalidates the fit of the point beneath it. Left alone, the apex
              climbed past stationary neighbours and the curve into it narrowed into a spike --
              the tip *sharpened on every cycle*, quietly undoing the rounded tip a reader had
              just picked over the pointed one.

              An earlier draft of this comment called that imprecision unavoidable, on the
              grounds that fixing it meant hand-keying four handle properties per vertex and
              `smooth.py` exists precisely so nobody hand-tunes cubic handles. The first half
              was true and the conclusion was wrong: `smooth.py` does not tune handles, it
              *fits* them, and a fitter can be run at every keyframe as easily as once. So it
              is, and the frame-0 values it emits match the authored ones to four decimals,
              which is the check that there is no step at the seam.

              Which vertices get handle blocks falls out rather than being chosen: the outer
              flame's other tip neighbours are index 0 and index 9, both `StraightVertex` with
              no handles, so only 1 and 7 appear; the core is a plain ring with no corners, so
              both of its apex's neighbours do.
            -->
